1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Нарышкин,_Лев_Кириллович_(1809—1855)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lev Kirillovich Naryshkin (August 20 (September 1) 1809 - September 20 (October 2) 1855) was a Russian courtier from the Naryshkin family, chief state marshal, chief master, chamberlain of the court, member of the State Council, o
2 CONC wner of the Sergievka estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT The eldest son of chamberlain Kirill Alexandrovich Naryshkin from his marriage to Prince Maria Yakovlevna Lobanova-Rostovskaya. Born in St. Petersburg, he was baptized on August 31, 1809 in the church of the Tauride Palace under t
2 CONC he reception of Emperor Alexander I and Empress Maria Feodorovna[1]. C. N. Marin was very friendly with the Naryshkins and dedicated poems to the baby: "The Whee (The Angel is cute, the handles are gentle...)." He got a home upbri
2 CONC nging.
2 CONT
2 CONT After completing the course at the Tsarskoye Selo Lyceum in 1826, he began his service in the department of the State College of Foreign Affairs and was included in our mission in Rome. Two years later, he entered military service
2 CONC , being adopted by the Highest Order on November 29, 1828 by the cornet to the Hussar Prince of Orange Regiment, as part of which he participated in the Turkish Campaign the following year. As an aide-de-camp to the commander-in-c
2 CONC hief of the 2nd Army, Field Marshal Count Dibić-Zabalikansky participated in many cases, among other things - under Silistria, Shumla, Adrianople, and for distinctions he was awarded the rank of lieutenant and the Orders of St. An
2 CONC na 3rd degree and St. Vladimir 4th station with a bow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Being rich, Naryshkin lived in his own St. Petersburg house on Palace Embankment, 20/2, where he gave "beautiful evenings and exclusive balls"[2]. According to a contemporary, he was "one of the most beautiful men, he had wonderfu
2 CONC l eyes, an interesting melancholic expression and something exquisite in his entire appearance"[3]. He was "a man of boiling activity, generous, truthful to the highest degree, fair, kind to tenderness, but when passion was excite
2 CONC d, he was indomitable, like all strong characters," Decembrist A. wrote about Naryshkin. P. Belyaev[4]. He died in 1855 in St. Petersburg and was buried in the cemetery of the Annunciation Church of the Alexander Nevsky Lavra. Aft
2 CONC er Naryshkin's death, his minor sons were under the care of E. D. Naryshkina.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife (since April 26, 1838) - Princess Maria Vasilyevna Dolgorukova (19.04.1814[5]-07.12.1869), lady-in-waiting of the court, daughter of Jaegermeister Vasily Vasilyevich Dolgorukov and goddaughter of Empress Maria Feodorovna. Acc
2 CONC ording to a contemporary, Madame Naryshkina "held on with an aplomb, with confidence and calmness" in society, she had "a wonderful face of the rarest beauty, a beautiful profile, correct and subtle features, very lively beautiful
2 CONC  black eyes, black hair and pale moonlight"[3], but also great oddities[6]. Like her brother, she suffered from insanity, from which she was treated in Bonn by the then famous mental illness doctor, where she was placed by her hus
2 CONC band. Later, against his will, even with the intervention of Emperor Nicholas I, it was brought from abroad and handed over to her father. After his death, she lived in Moscow under the care of her relative (sister's brother) Prin
2 CONC ce V. A. Dolgorukova. She was buried in St. Petersburg in the Fedorovskaya Church of the Alexander Nevsky Lavra. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT # Kirill (02.05.1839—04.10.1859)
2 CONT # Vasily (28.12.1841-25.05.1906), chamber-junker, rich man, known as a collector of paintings, works of decorative and applied art, weapons. He presented his assembly as a gift to Emperor Alexander II[7].
2 CONT
